    {
      "page_title": "test and [ ]: The POSIX Workhorse",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "No Globs, No Regex"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[ \"$f\" = *.txt ]` compares to the literal `*.txt`",
            "Pattern matching belongs to `[[ ]]` or `case`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Which Bracket",
          "text": "Shebang `#!/bin/sh` or dash: `[ ]` with every variable quoted. Shebang `#!/bin/bash`: reach for `[[ ]]`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Keyword, Not a Command",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bash parses `[[ ]]` specially — expansion rules suspended",
            "No word splitting, no globbing on variables",
            "`[[ $x = y ]]` is safe when `x` is empty or has spaces",
            "Cannot go where a command is needed, e.g. `find -exec`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "x=''",
            "[ $x = a ]    # error",
            "[[ $x = a ]]  # false, no error",
            "f='my file'; [[ -f $f ]]  # no quotes needed"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Patterns and Regex"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`==` / `!=` glob-match: `[[ $name == a* ]]`",
            "A quoted right side is literal: `[[ $f == \"*.txt\" ]]`",
            "`=~` takes an ERE, not PCRE, and must be UNQUOTED",
            "Capture groups land in the `BASH_REMATCH` array"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ $f == *.txt ]]        # glob match",
            "[[ $ip =~ ^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$ ]]",
            "echo \"${BASH_REMATCH[0]}\"  # whole match",
            "re='^v[0-9]+'; [[ $tag =~ $re ]]  # bare var"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "[0] whole match",
                "[1] group 1"
              ],
              [
                "[2] group 2",
                "[3] group 3"
              ]
            ],
            "caption": "BASH_REMATCH"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Escape Quoting Hell",
          "text": "Store a complex regex in a variable and use it bare. Quoting `$re` turns the pattern back into a literal string."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Operators Inside"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`&&`, `||` and `( )` grouping: `[[ -f $f && -r $f ]]`",
            "`<` and `>` compare strings, no backslash escaping",
            "`-eq` / `-lt` still work, but `(( ))` is the better choice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Top-3 Real Bug"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ 10 > 9 ]]   # FALSE - string compare",
            "(( 10 > 9 ))   # true - arithmetic",
            "[[ 10 -gt 9 ]] # true, but noisy"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "[[ ]]: Bash's Safer Conditional Keyword",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Choosing the Bracket"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Splits",
            "Regex",
            "Best for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`[ ]`",
              "yes",
              "no",
              "POSIX `sh`"
            ],
            [
              "`[[ ]]`",
              "no",
              "yes",
              "Bash strings"
            ],
            [
              "`(( ))`",
              "n/a",
              "no",
              "numbers"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Rules of Thumb",
          "items": [
            "Left side: quoting never needed",
            "Right side unquoted = pattern; quoted = literal",
            "`${BASH_REMATCH[1]}` is the first capture group"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Forms, Two Jobs",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`(( expr ))` evaluates and sets an exit status",
            "`$(( expr ))` is EXPANSION: substitutes the result",
            "Variables need no `$` inside: `(( i < n ))`",
            "`$` is allowed there but only adds noise"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Inverted Truth",
          "text": "A numeric result of `0` gives exit status `1` (false); non-zero gives status `0` (true). The opposite of everywhere else — memorise it."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "(( 0 )); echo $?   # 1  - false",
            "(( 5 )); echo $?   # 0  - true",
            "n=3; echo $(( n * 2 ))   # 6"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Operator Set"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "C-style: `+ - * / % **`, `++ --`, `+= -= *= /=`",
            "Ternary `? :`, logical `&& || !`",
            "Bitwise `& | ^ << >>`",
            "Real chaining: `(( a < b && b < c ))`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Integer Only"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$(( 7 / 2 ))` is `3` — truncating division",
            "`$(( 1.5 ))` is a syntax error; use `bc -l` or `awk`",
            "`$(( -7 % 3 ))` is `-1`, truncated toward zero",
            "Overflow wraps at 64-bit signed, silently, no error"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Octal Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "echo $(( 010 ))    # 8 - leading zero is octal",
            "echo $(( 08 + 1 )) # value too great for base",
            "m=08; echo $(( 10#$m ))  # 8, forced base 10",
            "echo $(( ${m#0} ))       # or strip the zero"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bites date math: `08` and `09` are invalid octal digits",
            "Base-N literals: `2#1011`=11, `16#ff`=255, `8#17`=15"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Arithmetic: (( )) and $(( ))",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "set -e and ++"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "i=0",
            "(( i++ ))     # status 1 kills a set -e script",
            "(( i++ )) || true   # safe",
            "(( ++i ))     # pre-inc returns the new value"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Post-increment returns the OLD value's truthiness",
            "`let 'x = 1 + 2'` and `declare -i x` are older forms",
            "`(( ))` is the modern idiom — prefer it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Guard Idiom",
          "text": "`if (( count > 0 )); then` reads better and is safer than `[ \"$count\" -gt 0 ]`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Word-List Form",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for x in a b c` iterates words after expansion",
            "An empty list means zero iterations, status `0`",
            "The loop returns its last body command's status"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "for x in a b c; do echo \"$x\"; done",
            "for f in *.txt; do wc -l \"$f\"; done",
            "for arg in \"$@\"; do echo \"$arg\"; done",
            "for arg; do echo \"$arg\"; done  # same thing"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Globs Beat ls"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for f in *.txt` is SAFE with spaces in filenames",
            "`for f in $(ls)` word-splits and breaks on newlines",
            "Never parse `ls` — the glob already did the work"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Empty Glob",
          "text": "With no matches, `*.txt` stays literal and the body runs once on the pattern itself. Guard with `[[ -e $f ]]` or `shopt -s nullglob`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Arrays and Ranges"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for x in \"${arr[@]}\"` — quotes and `[@]` both required",
            "`${arr[*]}` joins everything into one word",
            "Braces: `{1..10}`, step `{0..20..5}`, padded `{01..12}`",
            "`{1..$n}` fails: braces expand before parameters"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "for i in {1..10}; do echo $i; done",
            "for i in {0..20..5}; do echo $i; done",
            "for (( i=0; i<n; i++ )); do echo $i; done"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "C-Style and Control"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for (( i=0; i<n; i++ ))` is arithmetic context, no `$`",
            "The best form for counters and variable bounds",
            "`break` exits, `continue` skips; `break 2` exits two loops"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "for Loops: Lists, Globs, C-Style",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lines Are Not Words"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for line in $(cat f)` splits on every space, not per line",
            "`IFS=$'\\n'` narrows splitting but still globs",
            "Both stay inferior to `while IFS= read -r line`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Redirect the Whole Loop",
          "items": [
            "Loops are compound commands",
            "`for ...; do ...; done > out.txt` opens the file once",
            "Beats reopening it inside the body each pass"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Directions",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "while CMD",
                "lines": [
                  "repeats while status 0",
                  "stops on failure",
                  "`while (( i < 5 ))`"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "until CMD",
                "lines": [
                  "repeats while non-zero",
                  "stops on success",
                  "`until nc -z db 5432`"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The condition is a command list, not an expression",
            "`while [[ $i -lt 5 ]]`, `while (( i < 5 ))`, `while ping -c1 h`",
            "`until` is just `while !` — pick the positive reading"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Counters and Infinity"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "i=0",
            "while (( i<5 )); do echo $i; (( i++ )); done",
            "while true; do :; done   # infinite",
            "while :; do :; done      # ':' is the no-op"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Multi-command condition: only the LAST status decides",
            "`while cmd1; cmd2; do` branches on `cmd2`",
            "`break` / `continue` work as in `for`, with level counts"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "while and until: Condition-Driven Loops",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Retry and Poll"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "n=0",
            "until deploy; do",
            "  (( ++n >= 5 )) && exit 1",
            "  sleep $(( 2 ** n ))   # backoff 2,4,8,16",
            "done"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Always Sleep When Polling",
          "text": "A bare `while ! test; do :; done` pins one CPU core at 100%. The `sleep` in a polling loop is mandatory, not decoration."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where while Breaks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Infinite-loop bug: forgetting to mutate the counter",
            "Or mutating a copy inside a subshell — lost on exit",
            "`while read` consumes stdin; body commands fight for it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Stdin Thieves",
          "items": [
            "`ssh`, `ffmpeg` and `mysql` eat the loop's input",
            "The loop then exits after one iteration",
            "Fix with `ssh -n`, or `< /dev/null` on the command"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Keep the Body Here"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while read x; do n=$x; done < <(cmd)  # stays",
            "cmd | while read x; do n=$x; done  # subshell"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shape of a case",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "case $var in",
            "  start|begin|up) run ;;",
            "  -*) die \"unknown $var\" ;;",
            "  *) usage ;;",
            "esac"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$var` needs no quotes — no splitting in the case word",
            "Same protection you get inside `[[ ]]`",
            "First matching pattern wins; no fallthrough by default",
            "No `break` needed — `;;` ends each branch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Globs, Not Regex"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Pattern",
            "Matches"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`*`",
              "any string"
            ],
            [
              "`?`",
              "exactly one char"
            ],
            [
              "`[abc]`",
              "one of a, b, c"
            ],
            [
              "`[!abc]`",
              "anything else"
            ],
            [
              "`a|b`",
              "alternation"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A quoted pattern is literal: `\"*\"` means an asterisk",
            "Otherwise patterns match the expanded word directly",
            "`shopt -s extglob` adds `@(a|b) ?(x) *(x) +(x) !(x)`"
          ]
        }
      ]
